igh-quality critiques for supervised fine-tuning, after which the fine-tuned model generates its own critiques for reward learning, mitigating distribution shift between training and inference. On an in-domain test set of 200 SongEval songs, MuseCritic reduces macro-averaged mean squared error from 0.2875 to 0.2316 and improves macro-averaged LCC, SRCC, and Kendall’s τ \\tau to 0.9068, 0.8838, and 0.7178, respectively. On the out-of-domain Music Arena benchmark with 733 preference \n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b190414d-f9c9-4bab-ae46-c03d70338d10","uuid":"4d9da611-a1db-4ffc-8aac-acbea234ab64","timestamp":"2026-08-13T07:39:32.451Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"MuseCritic: Learning Multi-Aspect Song Rewards through Natural-Language Aesthetic Critiques Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Submit without GitHub Submit in GitHub arXiv is now an independent nonprofit! Learn more &times; Back to arXiv License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license arXiv:2608.11755v1 [cs.SD] 12 Aug 2026 † † * Equal contribution. † Corresponding authors MuseCritic: Learning Multi-Aspect Song Rewards through Natural-Language Aesthetic Critiques Jiabao Zhuang *,† , Changhao Jiang * , Hanchen Wang * , Jiahao Chen * , Zhixiong Yang * , Zhenghao Xiang * , Yifei Cao, Jiajun Sun, Hui Li, Ming Zhang, Tao Ji, Tao Gui † , Qi Zhang, Xuanjing Huang Fudan NLP Group, Fudan University 25213050071@m.fudan.edu.cn, tgui@fudan.edu.cn Abstract Long-form song generation models continue to improve in duration, structural integrity, and acoustic complexity, making reliable aesthetic rewards increasingly important for aligning these models with human preferences. However, reward models for complete songs remain limited, and existing evaluators typically predict scores in a single forward pass without providing readable explanations. To this end, we introduce MuseCritic, a semi-scalar reward model that generates a natural-language critique covering five aesthetic dimensions and uses it as an intermediate representation to predict continuous reward scores. MuseCritic follows a two-stage training pipeline: a teacher model first provides high-quality critiques for supervised fine-tuning, after which the fine-tuned model generates its own critiques for reward learning, mitigating distribution shift between training and inference. On an in-domain test set of 200 SongEval songs, MuseCritic reduces macro-averaged mean squared error from 0.2875 to 0.2316 and improves macro-averaged LCC, SRCC, and Kendall’s τ \\tau to 0.9068, 0.8838, and 0.7178, respectively. On the out-of-domain Music Arena benchmark with 733 preference pairs, it achieves the highest accuracy of 71.35%. Moreover, using MuseCritic with GRPO improves Muse-0.6B on all nine aesthetic metrics from SongEval and Audiobox Aesthetics. These results demonstrate that critique-conditioned reward modeling reduces scoring error and provides an effective optimization signal for song generation. The project repository is available at https://github.com/WuqnEl/MuseCritic . 1 Introduction Text-to-music generation has progressed from synthesizing short clips to composing complete songs. Early systems generate musical excerpts from natural-language descriptions ( 1 ; 5 ) , while recent systems—including DiffRhythm, YuE, LeVo, ACE-Step, and Muse—support minutes-long vocal music, lyric alignment, and fine-grained style control ( 17 ; 25 ; 14 ; 7 ; 12 ) . As generation length and acoustic realism improve, reliable aesthetic evaluation becomes increasingly important for model selection and for providing optimization signals aligned with human preferences. Existing evaluators, however, remain limited for complete songs. Objective metrics such as phoneme error rate, MuLan, and MuQ-MuLan capture lyric intelligibility or audio–text correspondence rather than perceptual and artistic quality ( 11 ; 28 ) . PAM, MusicEval, and Audiobox Aesthetics broaden evaluation to audio quality and musical aesthetics ( 6 ; 15 ; 20 ) , while SongEval provides five expert aesthetic criteria for full-length synthetic songs ( 23 ) ; nevertheless, these evaluators map audio directly to scores and do not explain the audible evidence behind their judgments. In contrast, LLM-as-a-Judge and critique-conditioned reward models show that language models can interpret rubrics and generate corresponding rationales or supporting reasoning before scoring textual responses, thereby improving textual preference modeling ( 27 ; 2 ; 24 ; 26 ; 16 ) . Whether this linguistic intermediate representation can improve multidimensional aesthetic reward modeling for complete songs remains underexplored. Existing audio reward models predict scores directly, while general-purpose multimodal models lack task-specific training and show weaker agreement with expert judgments. Figure 1: Overview of the two-stage training and inference pipeline of MuseCritic. In Stage I, Gemini-3-Pro generates aesthetic critiques from songs, evaluation rubrics, and expert mean ratings to construct 𝒟 off \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{off}} , which is used to fine-tune a pretrained large language model into an SFT critique generator. In Stage II, the SFT model replaces the teacher critiques with one self-generated five-aspect critique per song to construct 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} ; a scalar reward head is then initialized, and MuseCritic is trained using expert mean ratings as regression targets. At inference time, MuseCritic first generates a five-aspect critique and then predicts five continuous aesthetic scores conditioned on the song, rubric, and self-generated critique. In this work, we investigate explicit linguistic reasoning in song reward modeling, enabling the model to form a representation of aesthetic evidence aligned with the evaluation rubric before predicting multidimensional scores. To this end, we propose MuseCritic, a semi-scalar reward model built around natural-language aesthetic critiques. Given a song and a five-dimensional evaluation rubric, MuseCritic generates a single critique containing five labeled analyses: Overall Coherence, Memorability, Naturalness of Vocal Breathing and Phrasing, Clarity of Song Structure, and Overall Musicality. Conditioned on the song, rubric, and self-generated critique, a reward head then predicts five continuous aesthetic scores ranging from 1 to 5. The critique is not a post-hoc text appended to predicted scores; it is an explicit intermediate variable connecting song representations to rewards. This design introduces a variable-length textual representation before prediction and organizes auditory evidence distributed across a long song into dimension-aligned descriptions. Figure 1 summarizes the training and inference pipeline. The model first learns five-dimensional aesthetic analysis from critiques generated by an external teacher. It then generates self-generated critiques for the training songs and performs reward learning on the resulting dataset. Across both in-domain and out-of-domain evaluations, critique-conditioned reward modeling consistently improves agreement with human judgments. On the held-out SongEval test set, MuseCritic reduces macro-averaged MSE from 0.2875 to 0.2316 and raises macro-averaged LCC, SRCC, and Kendall’s τ \\tau to 0.9068, 0.8838, and 0.7178. On Music Arena, MuseCritic attains the highest observed pairwise accuracy of 71.35% across 733 preference pairs. When used as the reward model for GRPO, it further improves Muse-0.6B on all nine aesthetic metrics reported by SongEval and Audiobox Aesthetics. Our contributions are threefold: • We introduce MuseCritic, a semi-scalar reward model for complete songs. It generates a natural-language critique containing five dimension-specific analyses and uses this critique to predict five continuous scores, combining verbal evaluation and reward estimation within a single model (Figure 1 ). • We systematically evaluate MuseCritic on in-domain SongEval ratings and out-of-domain Music Arena preferences. Self-generated critiques improve agreement with expert scores and mitigate the critique distribution shift between reward training and inference (Tables 1 , 2 , and 4 ). • We use MuseCritic as the reward model for GRPO training of Muse-0.6B. The resulting policy improves on all nine observed metrics from SongEval and Audiobox Aesthetics, showing that the learned reward provides a useful optimization signal (Table 3 ). 2 Related Work 2.1 Text-to-Song Generation Advances in neural audio codecs and large-scale audio generation have moved language-conditioned music synthesis from early systems such as MusicLM and MusicGen toward recent systems capable of generating complete songs ( 1 ; 5 ) . Unlike short musical excerpts, complete songs require cross-section structure, coordination between vocals and accompaniment, and long-range lyric alignment. Recent systems can generate complete songs several minutes in duration. DiffRhythm performs end-to-end long-form song synthesis through latent diffusion ( 17 ) ; YuE combines dual-track tokenization with progressive structural conditioning to generate songs up to five minutes long ( 25 ) ; and ACE-Step integrates diffusion with musical semantic representations ( 7 ) . LeVo and Muse further support vocal–accompaniment coordination and fine-grained, multi-turn style control ( 14 ; 12 ) . MusicRL learns music rewards from human feedback ( 4 ) , while LeVo uses multi-preference DPO to improve song quality and instruction following ( 14 ) . As complete-song generation matures, reliable aesthetic evaluation and reward signals suitable for reinforcement learning become central challenges. Our work focuses on the reward itself, enabling one model to both describe five-dimensional aesthetic quality and assign continuous rewards. 2.2 Reward Modeling and Music Aesthetic Evaluation Classical RLHF reward models attach a shallow scalar head to a pretrained language-model backbone to fit human preferences ( 18 ) , whereas LLM-as-a-Judge uses capable language models to produce evaluations and supporting rationales ( 27 ) . Semi-scalar and generative reward models seek to combine linguistic explanation with numerical prediction. CLoud generates natural-language critiques before predicting rewards ( 2 ) ; Critic-RM jointly trains self-generated critiques and reward prediction ( 24 ) ; and Generative Verifiers and DeepSeek-GRM improve reward judgments through reasoning traces and test-time sampling ( 26 ; 16 ) . These approaches primarily evaluate text responses. MuseCritic extends this modeling paradigm to long-form song audio and multidimensional continuous aesthetic assessment. Existing music evaluators typically predict proxy metrics or scalar scores. MuLan and MuQ-MuLan measure text–music correspondence ( 11 ; 28 ) ; PAM estimates reference-free audio quality ( 6 ) ; MusicEval trains a CLAP-based evaluator on expert ratings of generated music clips ( 15 ) ; and Audiobox Aesthetics predicts content enjoyment, content usefulness, production complexity, and production quality ( 20 ) . SongEval defines five dimensions for complete Chinese and English songs: coherence, memorability, vocal naturalness, structural clarity, and musicality ( 23 ) , but still targets score prediction alone. MuseCritic adopts the SongEval rubric while retaining the critique-generation capability of an audio language model, allowing explicit aesthetic evidence to participate in reward prediction. 3 Method 3.1 Preliminaries Problem formulation. Let x ∈ 𝒳 x\\in\\mathcal{X} denote a complete song with vocals, p p an aesthetic-evaluation prompt that defines the assessment dimensions, and K = 5 K=5 the number of dimensions. The expert annotation is 𝐲 ∗ = [ y 1 ∗ , … , y K ∗ ] ∈ [ 1 , 5 ] K \\mathbf{y}^{*}=[y^{*}_{1},\\ldots,y^{*}_{K}]\\in[1,5]^{K} . The five dimensions are Overall Coherence, Memorability, Naturalness of Vocal Breathing and Phrasing, Clarity of Song Structure, and Overall Musicality ( 23 ) . We use an instruction-tuned audio language model f θ f_{\\theta} as the shared backbone of our semi-scalar reward model. Its critique generator g c g_{c} and aesthetic scorer g s g_{s} are g c = h LM ∘ f θ , g s = h RM ∘ f θ , g_{c}=h_{\\mathrm{LM}}\\circ f_{\\theta},\\qquad g_{s}=h_{\\mathrm{RM}}\\circ f_{\\theta}, (1) where h LM h_{\\mathrm{LM}} is a language-modeling head that generates a natural-language aesthetic critique, and h RM h_{\\mathrm{RM}} is a reward-modeling head that produces K K continuous aesthetic scores. The two components always share the same backbone parameters θ \\theta . Reward modeling. Unlike reward models that predict a scalar directly from audio, the aesthetic scorer of MuseCritic jointly conditions on the song x x , evaluation rubric p p , and aesthetic critique c c : 𝐲 ^ = g s ​ ( x , p , c ) . \\hat{\\mathbf{y}}=g_{s}(x,p,c). (2) For any set 𝒮 \\mathcal{S} of expert-annotated samples, we learn the five-dimensional scores by minimizing mean squared error: ℒ MSE ​ ( 𝒮 , g s ) \\displaystyle\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{MSE}}(\\mathcal{S};g_{s}) = 1 | 𝒮 | ​ K ∑ ( x , p , c , 𝐲 ∗ ) ∈ 𝒮 \\displaystyle=\\frac{1}{|\\mathcal{S}|K}\\sum_{(x,p,c,\\mathbf{y}^{*})\\in\\mathcal{S}} (3) ‖ g s ​ ( x , p , c ) − 𝐲 ∗ ‖ 2 2 . \\displaystyle\\left\\|g_{s}(x,p,c)-\\mathbf{y}^{*}\\right\\|_{2}^{2}. This objective simultaneously constrains all five dimensions to align the predictions with expert ratings. 3.2 The MuseCritic Reward Model Overview. To introduce an explicit textual intermediate representation into reward modeling, we treat natural-language aesthetic critiques as intermediate variables between a song and its final scores. Given a song x x and rubric p p , MuseCritic first generates c ^ = g c ​ ( x , p ) , \\hat{c}=g_{c}(x,p), (4) where c ^ \\hat{c} is a single critique with five labeled sections describing perceptible musical phenomena under the corresponding dimensions. The shared backbone then receives the song, rubric, and self-generated critique to produce 𝐲 ^ = g s ​ ( x , p , c ^ ) . \\hat{\\mathbf{y}}=g_{s}(x,p,\\hat{c}). (5) Relative to direct score prediction, Equation 5 introduces a variable-length textual representation before scoring and organizes descriptions of auditory evidence distributed across a long song according to the evaluation rubric. Training MuseCritic. Ideally, MuseCritic would be trained on a dataset 𝒟 ∗ = { ( x i , p , c i ∗ , 𝐲 i ∗ ) } i = 1 N \\mathcal{D}^{*}=\\{(x_{i},p,c_{i}^{*},\\mathbf{y}_{i}^{*})\\}_{i=1}^{N} , where c i ∗ c_{i}^{*} is a human-authored aesthetic critique consistent with the corresponding expert ratings. Such critiques would be authored by human music experts, but existing song datasets rarely pair expert scores with expert critiques. Following prior work that constructs supervision from model feedback ( 3 ; 2 ) , we use the audio-capable Gemini-3-Pro ( 9 ) to generate score-conditioned synthetic critiques. Specifically, we begin with a score-only dataset 𝒟 score = { ( x i , 𝐲 i ∗ ) } i = 1 N \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{score}}=\\{(x_{i},\\mathbf{y}_{i}^{*})\\}_{i=1}^{N} . To generate a critique, Gemini observes the song x i x_{i} , rubric p p , and expert mean ratings 𝐲 i ∗ \\mathbf{y}_{i}^{*} , then writes dimension-specific commentary whose evaluative polarity is consistent with each rating on a five-point Likert scale. The prompt requests analysis of concrete musical phenomena while prohibiting numerical scores in the critique itself. This procedure yields the offline critique dataset 𝒟 off = { ( x i , p , c i off , 𝐲 i ∗ ) } i = 1 N . \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{off}}=\\{(x_{i},p,c_{i}^{\\mathrm{off}},\\mathbf{y}_{i}^{*})\\}_{i=1}^{N}. (6) Appendix D provides the complete external-teacher prompt. To further ensure the reliability of the aesthetic critiques, we manually verify the generated critiques, as detailed in Appendix A . Training comprises two stages. In Stage I, we supervise the audio language model on 𝒟 off \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{off}} with the critique-generation objective ℒ SFT = \\displaystyle\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{SFT}}={} − 1 ∑ i = 1 N | c i off | \\displaystyle-\\frac{1}{\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\lvert c_{i}^{\\mathrm{off}}\\rvert} (7) ∑ i = 1 N ∑ t = 1 | c i off | log ⁡ p θ ​ ( c i , t off ∣ x i , p , c i , &lt; t off ) . \\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{N}\\sum_{t=1}^{\\lvert c_{i}^{\\mathrm{off}}\\rvert}\\log p_{\\theta}\\!\\left(c_{i,t}^{\\mathrm{off}}\\mid x_{i},p,c_{i,&lt;t}^{\\mathrm{off}}\\right). This is the standard next-token cross-entropy averaged over non-masked critique target tokens; the audio and user-prompt tokens do not contribute to the loss. This stage produces a critique generator g c ′ g^{\\prime}_{c} with the ability to analyze a song along the five aesthetic dimensions. In Stage II, we first use g c ′ g^{\\prime}_{c} to generate a new critique for every training example: c i on = g c ′ ​ ( x i , p ) . c_{i}^{\\mathrm{on}}=g^{\\prime}_{c}(x_{i},p). (8) We replace the external-teacher critiques with these self-generated critiques and call the resulting collection the self-generated critique dataset 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} : 𝒟 on = { ( x i , p , c i on , 𝐲 i ∗ ) } i = 1 N . \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}}=\\{(x_{i},p,c_{i}^{\\mathrm{on}},\\mathbf{y}_{i}^{*})\\}_{i=1}^{N}. (9) We then initialize the shared MuseCritic backbone from the SFT model and jointly update the backbone LoRA parameters and all reward-head parameters on 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} . The reward model thus learns from critiques generated by the target model family rather than by the external teacher, mitigating the critique distribution shift between reward training and inference. The objective is the MSE from Equation 3 applied to 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} : ℒ MC = ℒ MSE ​ ( 𝒟 on , g s ) . \\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{MC}}=\\mathcal{L}_{\\mathrm{MSE}}(\\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}};g_{s}). (10) At inference time, the final LoRA-updated MuseCritic backbone generates an aesthetic critique according to Equation 4 and then predicts five rewards according to Equation 5 . 4 Experiments 4.1 Experimental Setup Data. We use SongEval ( 23 ) for training and in-domain evaluation. It contains 2,399 complete Chinese and English songs totaling more than 140 hours of audio, with five-dimensional aesthetic scores from 16 annotators with music expertise. The data span nine major genres, including pop, rock, and electronic music. Because the original work does not release a fixed train–test split, we shuffle all samples with seed 42, use 2,199 songs for training, and reserve the remaining 200 for testing. Every experiment uses this split. For the training set, Gemini-3-Pro ( 9 ) observes each song and its expert mean ratings to generate a single critique containing five dimension-specific analyses, producing 𝒟 off \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{off}} . The SFT model subsequently regenerates one five-part critique per song with greedy decoding ( T = 0 T=0 , no sampling), producing the self-generated critique dataset 𝒟 on \\mathcal{D}_{\\mathrm{on}} . Appendices D and A provide the teacher and model prompts and the splitting algorithm. For out-of-domain evaluation, we use the 733 song-preference pairs from Music Arena ( 13 ) . This dataset contains outputs from systems not represented in SongEval and provides pairwise human preferences rather than five-dimensional absolute scores. It therefore measures preference generalization under distribution shift. Baselines. The in-domain task requires five-dimensional song aesthetic scores. We compare against two classes of baselines: (1) Gemini-3.1-Pro ( 8 ) , an audio-capable LLM-as-a-Judge that generates critiques and five-dimensional ratings under the same rubric; and (2) SongEval (UTMOS) , a discriminative reward model that directly regresses five scores from audio. SongEval does not release its original training code or data split, so we reimplement its published method and UTMOS architecture and train it for ten epochs on our 2,199/200 split, controlling for differences in training data. Music Arena requires pairwise preference prediction. In addition to MuseCritic, we evaluate the officially released SongEval weights ( 23 ) , the general-purpose audio aesthetic model Audiobox Aesthetics ( 20 ) , and the open-source audio language model Qwen3-Omni-30B-A3B-Instruct ( 21 ) . Qwen3-Omni uses the same rubric and generative scoring format as the in-domain Gemini baseline. Metrics. For in-domain evaluation, we report dimension-wise mean squared error (MSE), Pearson’s linear correlation coefficient (LCC), Spearman’s rank correlation coefficient (SRCC), and Kendall’s τ \\tau (KTAU). MSE measures agreement with the experts’ absolute rating scale, while the three correlation coefficients measure relative agreement across songs in terms of linear association and ranking. Each metric is computed independently over the 200 test songs for each dimension. For out-of-domain evaluation, we report pairwise accuracy. A prediction is correct only when the model assigns a strictly higher overall reward to the human-preferred song; ties count as errors. For SongEval, Audiobox Aesthetics, and MuseCritic, we average the respective output dimensions with equal weight. For Qwen3-Omni, we parse its five generated ratings under the shared rubric and take their mean. All main evaluations use deterministic decoding with T = 0 T=0 . For downstream evaluation after GRPO, music quality is assessed using SongEval ( 23 ) in terms of overall coherence (CO), memorability (ME), naturalness of vocal breathing and phrasing (NA), clarity of song structure (CL), and overall musicality (MU). Audio quality is evaluated with Audiobox Aesthetics ( 20 ) , which considers content enjoyment (CE), content usefulness (CU), production complexity (PC), and production quality (PQ). Training. All MuseCritic variants use MOSS-Audio-8B-Instruct ( 22 ) as the base model. During critique cold-start, we fine-tune all backbone parameters with a learning rate of 5 × 10 − 5 5\\times 10^{-5} and use the checkpoint saved after one epoch. We initialize MuseCritic from this checkpoint and train it for ten epochs on the self-generated critique data. The backbone uses LoRA (rank 8, scaling factor 32) ( 10 ) , while the reward head is fully updated with a learning rate of 2 × 10 − 4 2\\times 10^{-4} . Both optimization stages use four NVIDIA H200 GPUs, a per-device batch size of 1, gradient accumulation over 8 steps (global batch size 32), a 5% warmup, cosine learning-rate scheduling, and a fixed random seed of 42. Reward learning uses a weight decay of 0.1. The offline-critique and no-critique ablations use exactly the same reward-learning configuration. Appendix C lists all hyperparameters. 4.2 Evaluation Results Dimension Model MSE ↓ \\downarrow LCC ↑ \\uparrow SRCC ↑ \\uparrow KTAU ↑ \\uparrow Coherence MuseCritic 0.2058 0.9147 0.8894 0.7262 Gemini-3.1-Pro 0.9881 0.5906 0.5898 0.4359 SongEval (UTMOS) 0.2605 0.8876 0.8605 0.6835 Memorability MuseCritic 0.2511 0.9098 0.8839 0.7232 Gemini-3.1-Pro 1.1183 0.5806 0.5623 0.4113 SongEval (UTMOS) 0.3055 0.8857 0.8595 0.6837 Vocal naturalness MuseCritic 0.2537 0.8906 0.8678 0.6945 Gemini-3.1-Pro 1.9736 0.4921 0.5237 0.3877 SongEval (UTMOS) 0.3071 0.8593 0.8293 0.6491 Structural clarity MuseCritic 0.2275 0.9068 0.8806 0.7109 Gemini-3.1-Pro 1.4740 0.5532 0.5752 0.4162 SongEval (UTMOS) 0.2730 0.8831 0.8565 0.6790 Musicality MuseCritic 0.2202 0.9120 0.8972 0.7344 Gemini-3.1-Pro 0.9763 0.6152 0.6099 0.4459 SongEval (UTMOS) 0.2913 0.8807 0.8599 0.6876 Table 1: In-domain five-dimensional aesthetic scoring on the SongEval test set. All models are evaluated on the fixed test set of 200 songs. SongEval (UTMOS) denotes the regressor retrained on the same 2,199/200 split. MSE measures absolute error against expert ratings; LCC, SRCC, and KTAU denote Pearson, Spearman, and Kendall τ \\tau correlations. Arrows indicate the preferred direction, and bold marks the best value for each metric and dimension. Model Accuracy (%) ↑ \\uparrow Qwen3-Omni-30B-A3B 53.75 Audiobox Aesthetics 68.49 SongEval 70.80 MuseCritic 71.35 Table 2: Out-of-domain pairwise preference results on Music Arena. The test set contains 733 song pairs with human preference labels. A prediction is correct when the model assigns a higher mean aesthetic score to the human-preferred song; ties count as errors. SongEval uses its officially released weights. MuseCritic achieves the highest observed accuracy. 4.2.1 In-Domain Song Aesthetic Scoring Table 1 compares model predictions with the expert mean ratings of the 200-song held-out SongEval test set. MuseCritic achieves the lowest MSE and highest correlation on all five dimensions. Relative to the SongEval (UTMOS) regression baseline trained on the same data, MuseCritic reduces macro-averaged MSE from 0.2875 to 0.2316. Macro-averaged LCC, SRCC, and KTAU increase from 0.8793, 0.8531, and 0.6766 to 0.9068, 0.8838, and 0.7178. Section 4.4 isolates the effect of critique modeling through controlled ablations. Although Gemini-3.1-Pro has not been specialized for song aesthetics, it generates coherent critiques. Its macro-average MSE of 1.3061, however, substantially exceeds the 0.2316 of MuseCritic, and its dimension-wise correlations fall below those of models supervised by expert scores. This result indicates that an unadapted LLM-as-a-Judge cannot reliably reproduce five-dimensional expert judgments of song aesthetics. By supervising the reward head with expert ratings, MuseCritic maps information in the critiques to continuous scores that better align with expert assessments. 4.2.2 Out-of-Domain Preference Generalization Table 2 reports pairwise preference accuracy on Music Arena. MuseCritic achieves the highest observed accuracy of 71.35%, which is 0.55, 2.86, and 17.60 percentage points above SongEval, Audiobox Aesthetics, and Qwen3-Omni, respectively. Together with the in-domain results, this observation suggests that the learned rewards retain ranking information under an out-of-domain preference evaluation. 4.3 Downstream Reinforcement Learning To test whether MuseCritic provides a useful optimization signal, we use it to train Muse-0.6B ( 12 ) with GRPO ( 19 ) . Using seed 42, we sample 500 multi-turn song-generation examples from the public Muse training set while preserving the original language ratio: 195 Chinese and 305 English examples. We define the reinforcement-learning reward function as the mean of the five aesthetic scores assigned by MuseCritic to each generated song x ~ \\tilde{x} : r ⁡ ( x ~ ) = 1 K ​ ∑ k = 1 K y ^ k ​ ( x ~ ) . r(\\tilde{x})=\\frac{1}{K}\\sum_{k=1}^{K}\\hat{y}_{k}(\\tilde{x}). (11) We train for one epoch on six H200 GPUs, sample eight outputs per prompt, and use a learning rate of 10 − 6 10^{-6} . Appendix F gives the complete rollout configuration. Evaluation uses the 100 multi-turn test prompts from Muse ( 12 ) . Both Muse and Muse-GRPO use greedy decoding with T = 0 T=0 . Appendix F describes the complete evaluation protocol and robustness checks across evaluation windows. As shown in Table 3 , Muse-GRPO obtains higher observed values than the original model on all nine Audiobox Aesthetics and SongEval metrics. The largest Audiobox gains occur in production complexity (PC, + 0.15 +0.15 ) and content enjoyment (CE, + 0.12 +0.12 ), while all five SongEval dimensions improve by 0.05 0.05 – 0.06 0.06 . The consistent numerical gains across two song-aesthetic evaluators suggest that the MuseCritic reward captures several aesthetic dimensions. Audiobox Aesthetics ↑ \\uparrow SongEval ↑ \\uparrow Model CE CU PC PQ CO MU ME CL NA Muse 7.33 7.64 6.37 8.11 4.01 3.84 3.94 3.89 3.83 Muse-GRPO 7.45 7.66 6.52 8.12 4.07 3.89 4.00 3.94 3.89 Table 3: Effect of MuseCritic-guided GRPO on the aesthetic quality of Muse-0.6B. Results use the 100 multi-turn Muse test prompts and T = 0 T=0 decoding. CE/CU/PC/PQ denote Audiobox content enjoyment, content usefulness, production complexity, and production quality. CO/MU/ME/CL/NA denote SongEval coherence, musicality, memorability, structural clarity, and vocal naturalness. Arrows indicate the preferred direction, and bold marks the better observed result in each column. Muse-GRPO yields numerical gains on all nine metrics. Figure 2: Effect of natural-language aesthetic critiques on five-dimensional score distributions. Curves show expert ratings, full MuseCritic predictions, and predictions from the no-critique variant on the fixed SongEval test set. Coherence, Musicality, Memorability, Clarity, and Naturalness correspond to the five aesthetic dimensions. Without critiques, predictions cluster in a narrow high-score region; the full model more closely matches the range of expert ratings. 4.4 Ablation Studies Table 4 studies three design choices across all five dimensions: natural-language aesthetic critiques, self-generated critiques, and SFT initialization before reward learning. All variants use the same training songs, reward head, and optimization configuration. Dimension Model variant MSE ↓ \\downarrow LCC ↑ \\uparrow SRCC ↑ \\uparrow KTAU ↑ \\uparrow Coherence MuseCritic 0.2058 0.9147 0.8894 0.7262 w/o critique 0.3881 0.9173 0.8954 0.7326 Offline critique 0.4821 0.8342 0.7566 0.5675 w/o SFT 0.8556 0.6300 0.6066 0.4689 Memorability MuseCritic 0.2511 0.9098 0.8839 0.7232 w/o critique 0.4936 0.9048 0.8842 0.7219 Offline critique 0.6635 0.8141 0.7590 0.5672 w/o SFT 1.0037 0.6175 0.6378 0.4823 Vocal naturalness MuseCritic 0.2537 0.8906 0.8678 0.6945 w/o critique 0.6186 0.8899 0.8684 0.7034 Offline critique 0.5739 0.7936 0.7527 0.5674 w/o SFT 0.6469 0.6779 0.6768 0.4978 Structural clarity MuseCritic 0.2275 0.9068 0.8806 0.7109 w/o critique 0.4635 0.9041 0.8785 0.7088 Offline critique 0.5033 0.8145 0.7479 0.5526 w/o SFT 0.6681 0.6869 0.6764 0.5061 Musicality MuseCritic 0.2202 0.9120 0.8972 0.7344 w/o critique 0.5386 0.9080 0.8875 0.7293 Offline critique 0.5175 0.8205 0.7765 0.5877 w/o SFT 0.5960 0.7354 0.7325 0.5488 Table 4: In-domain ablations of the key MuseCritic training choices. All variants use the same 2,199/200 SongEval split. “w/o critique” predicts scores directly, “Offline critique” trains on Gemini critiques, and “w/o SFT” generates training critiques with the unadapted MOSS-Audio model. The full model combines SFT initialization with self-generated critiques. Arrows indicate the preferred direction, and bold marks the best value for each metric and dimension. Self-generated aesthetic critiques improve absolute-score ag","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/inspire/qb-ilm/project/video-generation/chenxie-25019/hyr/iHateTheWorld555.github.io/.claude-review/projects/-inspire-qb-ilm-project-video-generation-chenxie-25019-hyr-iHateTheWorld555-github-io/b190414d-f9c9-4bab-ae46-c03d70338d10/tool-results/bu2oduroj.txt","persistedOutputSize":61055}}
